WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.039 --> 00:00:03.329
Today I'm talking to you from somewhere that's pretty weird for me

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:10.590
Just off to the side behind me is my old primary school and it's somewhere I haven't visited for like 11 years now

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.920
So being back here sort of makes me think a bit about

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:19.049
how much I've changed and learned since I was a student here and I

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.649
Feel like the different stages of my life

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:28.199
I've really become a different person like when I was at school

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:35.999
I wasn't interested at all in science through like all of primary school most of high school, I had no ambitions to I

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:43.709
don't know, do anything academic or go into science and and now science is basically my life. It was this sort of

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.500
Happy accident in a way that I got

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:50.670
interested into something and I think it was actually for me

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.699
just watching documentaries on TV. That's the only place I really learnt about science

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:00.389
or learnt about the interesting things behind it. And just from there

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:05.760
I had this passion and this interest and I feel really lucky to have had that because that's driven a lot of my

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.280
career choices and study choices

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.920
ever since and

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.790
Now science is kind of like my entire identity

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:22.799
it's who I am, a lot of my friends are scientists and I feel like some of them most interesting and

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:30.570
motivations in my life are to do with figuring out the world around us. Now, pursuing something with such an interest is

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:34.258
not, I think, without some sort of sacrifice

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:42.089
So I was never cool or particularly popular at a high school. Like I have a bit of a following now on things like YouTube

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:44.970
People here like hearing about physics

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:51.329
but probably not so much at school and I sort of was a bit of an odd person out there. I didn't

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.329
really have other friends that were super into physics.

00:01:54.329 --> 00:02:01.199
In fact, I didn't meet friends that were really like me until I got to uni. But I'll talk more about that later,

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.350
I just want to say

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:10.499
that I sort of feel it's a shame that being seen to pursue something like science

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:17.459
is kind of a nerdy pursuit and it's something that you're not going to be cool doing.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:19.800
But I was fine with that

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:20.970
and in fact

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:25.919
I'm actually really grateful now that I was really sort of shy at high school because if

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.939
I'd ever felt like it was possible for me to be in that

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:37.949
coolest group, almost popular, then I probably wouldn't have gone for science or wouldn't have been really

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:42.059
studious about it because I would have probably cared more about

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:48.569
maintaining some sort of cool identity or trying to fit in. I definitely did things that

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.200
were against the idea of fitting in so I

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.110
remember in high school going to the library and

00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:01.739
whilst everyone was sort of playing and joking around I would actually go and pick up like a science book

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:07.199
I remember picking up Stephen Hawking's Brief History of Time or New Scientist magazines and actually just

00:03:08.139 --> 00:03:13.619
wanting to sit by myself and read them and being okay with that and being okay with the fact that

00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:18.779
doing that made me sort of seem like a bit of an outcast in a way.

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:21.280
but

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:28.679
I've always rathered stay at home and read a physics book than go to a party or to go and get drunk and I

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.409
Guess I'm not saying that you need to make those sort of sacrifices, like that

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:38.309
you can't drink and party and study physics. Of course you can.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:45.509
But I guess in some ways for me pursuing something so intensely as I have science

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:51.279
did come with those sacrifices, but they didn't feel like sacrifices at the time because I

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.830
had no interest in

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.270
partying and going out.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:57.639
I think one of the reasons that I

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:04.089
love YouTube so much and that I wanted to get involved on this platform is because this was one of the first places that I

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:06.969
did find people like me. I was

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:11.079
Really into the whole Nerdfighteria thing

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:17.230
So I was a follower of the Vlogbrothers and I found people there that were also like it wasn't just physics

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:23.259
but they are really into like certain subjects and they were proud to call themselves nerds in a way and

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:29.589
To not care about what other people thought of them and to just pursue what they were interested in and have intellectual

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:34.149
conversations and talk about just interesting stuff without being judged and

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:35.990
Finding that

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:42.609
community on YouTube because I didn't really have that in real life was a big thing for me and a big thing for my

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:43.940
learning and

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.170
Being okay with being a nerd. In fact, I loved that term.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.709
I just wanted to find others that were also fine with it.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.040
Now that was school and I did go to a pretty small school

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.559
But it wasn't until I went to uni

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:02.080
And was now majoring in physics that I found all these other people that were interested in physics, too

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:07.959
and it was sort of mind-blowing for me because I had never been able to have that before and I was

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:12.160
Talking to people who also watch the same youtube channels as me

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:18.669
They watched like Veritasium and Numberphile and they loved those and I was like, wow, I can actually talk about these things for someone in

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:24.639
real life because I'd never really spoken to people about it at school because I just I don't know I didn't I

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:32.049
didn't know people that were as passionate about science as I was. It was just so cool to be

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:38.890
surrounded by he's nerdy conversations and for it to finally feel just accepted and

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:45.219
to no longer feel like I was making a choice to be a bit of an outcast by pursuing this.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:49.359
I was just sort of in my place and it felt really good.

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:54.789
So, I have had some comments from a few you guys that have been like you know, how do I

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.099
pursue something that I'm passionate about whether it's physics maths computer science something

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:01.889
But that I get teased for

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.509
for being interested in or that my parents don't support or my friends think I'm crazy

00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:10.589
and I guess what I'm trying to say, is that just

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:15.059
If you know you're interested in something try not to care about

00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:22.979
what that does to your social ranking or what your friends think of it at the time because as you go on to uni

00:06:22.979 --> 00:06:27.419
Or get more specialized you'll find people that think exactly the same as you.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:33.929
You will always find friends, if you look hard enough, who will accept you for your interests and

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:37.619
You know if you keep going and just stick with it

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:42.090
You'll get what I found which was to be surrounded by people who

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:47.039
are embracing the nerdy things and you're no longer the nerd it's just how it is.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.789
So just keep going just keep pursuing what you know

00:06:50.789 --> 00:06:57.269
you're interested in and eventually you will find other people that accept you for that and who share your interests

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.270
It's an amazing feeling to find that

00:07:00.009 --> 00:07:06.899
But it can be a long road until you do so just keep going and and talk to other people here in the comments

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.900
I'll try to answer as many as I can

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:15.359
But I hope that people here feel like they can talk to each other about some of these things and that you're not alone in

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.699
Having, I guess, pretty nerdy interests.

